# Haryana History and Formation: From Rakhigarhi to 1 November 1966

> Few states pack as much Indian history into so little ground as
> Haryana. A Harappan metropolis, the Mahabharata battlefield, Harsha's
> capital, three decisive battles at Panipat and a martyr-king of 1857 all
> lie within a few hours' drive of each other. The HSSC CET and Haryana
> Police papers draw history questions from exactly this list, and the
> formation date of the state is asked almost every cycle.

## 1. Ancient Haryana: Harappan Towns and the Kuru Land

The plains drained by the old Ghaggar-Saraswati channel carried some of
the largest settlements of the Indus (Harappan) civilisation.

| Site | District (older name) | Why it is asked |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Rakhigarhi | Hisar (now Hansi district) | Among the largest Harappan sites in the subcontinent; 2019 ancient-DNA study published in the journal Cell |
| Banawali | Fatehabad | Fortified town, grid-like streets, pre-Harappan to post-Harappan phases; excavated by R.S. Bisht (ASI) in 1970s |
| Bhirrana | Fatehabad | Very early (pre-Harappan) levels |
| Kunal | Fatehabad | Early Harappan site, silver ornaments reported |
| Mitathal | Bhiwani | Harappan and later layers |
| Farmana | Rohtak | Large Harappan burial ground |
| Agroha | Hisar | Ancient mound, linked with the Agrawal community's tradition |

[definition]
**Pre-Harappan, Mature Harappan, Late Harappan** are the three broad
phases used by archaeologists. Banawali is a textbook example because its
layers show all three, which is why examiners use it as the "stratified
site" in Haryana.
[/definition]

**Vedic and epic Haryana.** The land between the rivers Saraswati and
Drishadvati was called **Brahmavarta** in the later law-books, the region
where Vedic rites were believed to have been perfected. Most of today's
Haryana belonged to the **Kuru** janapada, which became a mahajanapada
around the sixth century BCE. **Kurukshetra** is the Mahabharata
battlefield, and **Jyotisar** is where tradition places the Gita's
teaching. Tradition also links Panipat (Panduprastha) and Sonipat
(Svarnaprastha) with the five villages the Pandavas demanded.

## 2. Mauryan, Gupta and the Harsha Era

| Period | Haryana link |
| --- | --- |
| Mauryan (c. 321-185 BCE) | Ashokan pillars at Topra (Yamunanagar) and Agroha (Hisar); the Topra pillar was moved to Delhi by Firoz Shah Tughlaq |
| Gupta (c. 4th-6th century CE) | Region lay in the empire's northwestern reach; coins and sculptures are found across the state |
| Pushyabhuti dynasty | Capital at **Thanesar (Sthaneshvara)**; **Harsha** ruled 606-647 CE and later moved his capital to Kannauj |
| Foreign visitor | The Chinese pilgrim **Xuanzang** visited Thanesar during Harsha's reign |

Harsha's court poet **Banabhatta** wrote the *Harshacharita*, which describes
Thanesar. Remember the pair "Thanesar - Harsha" and "Kannauj - later capital".

## 3. Medieval Haryana: Tarain, Panipat and Hemu

After Harsha, the Tomaras and then the Chauhans controlled the region.
Tarain (Taraori, in Karnal district) saw two linked battles.

| Battle | Year | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First Battle of Tarain | 1191 | Prithviraj Chauhan defeated Muhammad Ghori |
| Second Battle of Tarain | 1192 | Muhammad Ghori defeated Prithviraj Chauhan; Sultanate rule spreads in north India |

Hansi, Hisar and Sirsa later served as important Sultanate frontier towns;
**Firoz Shah Tughlaq** founded **Hisar-e-Firoza** (Hisar) in the 1350s and
dug canals from the Yamuna and Ghaggar to feed it.

Panipat gave India three battles on one plain:

| Battle | Year | Winner | Loser |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| First | 1526 | Babur | Ibrahim Lodi |
| Second | 5 November 1556 | Akbar (under Bairam Khan) | Hemu |
| Third | January 1761 | Ahmad Shah Abdali | Marathas under Sadashivrao Bhau |

[example]
**Hemu (Hemchandra Vikramaditya)** grew up in Rewari. He fought for the
Sur rulers, defeated Akbar's forces at Tughlaqabad and took Delhi in
October 1556, assuming the title Vikramaditya. Weeks later he was
defeated at Panipat, reportedly after an arrow struck his eye. Exams ask
the chain: Rewari - Hemu - Delhi 1556 - Second Panipat.
[/example]

Babur's win is the standard marker for the start of the **Mughal empire**,
and it is also remembered for the first major use of field artillery in
north India.

## 4. 1857 in Haryana

The uprising touched most of present Haryana. Ambala Cantonment saw
early unrest among sepoys in May 1857, and Hisar, Hansi, Sirsa, Rohtak,
Jhajjar and Rewari were all affected.

| Leader | Place | Anchor fact |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Rao Tula Ram | Rewari (Ahirwal) | Raised a force in 1857; fought the British at **Nasibpur (Narnaul)** on 16 November 1857; later died in Kabul in 1863 |
| Raja Nahar Singh | Ballabhgarh | Allied with Bahadur Shah Zafar; hanged at Chandni Chowk on 9 January 1858 |
| Nawab Abdur Rahman Khan | Jhajjar | Ruler who joined the revolt; his state was dissolved afterwards |
| Sadruddin and Mewati rebels | Mewat | Local resistance in the Mewat hills |

After the revolt the British moved the Delhi territory, including the
Haryana districts, under the **Punjab** administration (1858). That
administrative link is the seed of the 1966 split.

Haryana observes 9 January as the martyrdom day of Raja Nahar Singh, and
Rao Tula Ram is remembered with a postage stamp (2001) and an annual
fair at Rampura, Rewari.

## 5. Formation of Haryana: 1 November 1966

Demand for a Hindi-speaking state grew alongside the Punjabi Suba
movement. In 1966 the Centre decided to divide the state of Punjab on
language lines.

| Step | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Shah Commission | Set up in April 1966 under Justice J.C. Shah to draw the line between Punjabi- and Hindi-speaking areas; reported in May 1966 |
| Punjab Reorganisation Act | Passed by Parliament on 18 September 1966 |
| Date of formation | **1 November 1966**, the 17th state of the Indian Union |
| Chandigarh | Made a **Union Territory** and the joint capital of Punjab and Haryana |
| Courts | One common **Punjab and Haryana High Court** at Chandigarh |
| Hill areas | Pahari-majority areas of old Punjab went to Himachal Pradesh |
| First Chief Minister | **Bhagwat Dayal Sharma** (1 November 1966) |
| First Governor | **Dharma Vira** (1 November 1966) |

The **Haryana Public Service Commission** (HPSC) also began functioning on
1 November 1966.

Haryana Day (1 November) is the state's foundation day. Remember that
**Haryana did not get its own capital**: Chandigarh serves both states and
is administered by the Centre.

## 6. Commonly Confused Pairs and Memory Tricks

- Tarain: Prithviraj wins in 1191 (first), loses in 1192 (second). Trick: "91 win, 92 lose".
- Panipat order by winner: Babur, Akbar, Abdali. Trick: B-A-A (Babur, Akbar, Abdali).
- Rakhigarhi (Hisar/Hansi) versus Banawali (Fatehabad): the biggest site is Rakhigarhi.
- Thanesar is Harsha's early capital; Kannauj is his later one.
- Rao Tula Ram (Rewari, Nasibpur) versus Raja Nahar Singh (Ballabhgarh, hanged in Delhi).
- 18 September 1966 is the date of the Act; 1 November 1966 is the date of coming into being.

[summary]
Haryana's story runs from Harappan Rakhigarhi and Banawali, through the
Kuru land of Kurukshetra and Harsha's Thanesar, to Tarain (1191, 1192) and
the three Panipat battles (1526, 1556, 1761). In 1857, Rao Tula Ram and
Raja Nahar Singh led local resistance. The state was created on 1 November
1966 under the Punjab Reorganisation Act, 1966, with Chandigarh as a Union
Territory and shared capital, Bhagwat Dayal Sharma as first Chief
Minister and Dharma Vira as first Governor.
[/summary]
